t the next  several decades  in 1950 clark actually wrote a short  book called  interplanetary flight which outlined the  basic concept of space travel to people  who weren't  necessarily familiar with the idea of  course this was based on their current  understanding  of astronautics at the time so not  exactly accurate today  arthur c clarke in particular made  several predictions about subjects  related to  but not directly tied to space travel  for example clark predicted the eventual  existence of telecommunications  satellites  the writers of the last couple of  hundred years especially the 40s  50s and 60s are the ones we remember  most when considering the history of  space travel and stories  but actually the idea of mankind  traveling through space  is much much older ancient in fact  there are hindu stories from the  ramayana which  is one of two major sanskrit epics  depicting flying machines capable of  destroying cities with advanced weapons  of mass destruction  and traveling into space these date back  to the 4th  and 5th century between 300 and 500 a.d  another example of an ancient text  depicting space travel that we have to  mention  is lucien of samosata's second century  novilla  or short novel depending on who you ask  true history  in authentic science fiction fashion  true history uses the setting of a  voyage through outer space  to make a statement on the use of  hyperbole in travel literature  the novella opens by explaining that the  story itself  is not in fact true and that everything  in it is in fact  a lie i write of matters which i neither  saw nor suffered  nor heard by reports from others which  are in no being nor possible  ever to have a beginning this depending  on how you look at it  might beg the question is that statement  itself a lie  but then that would be a paradox and it  gets messier from there but that's not  the point  and the novel we encounter alien life  forms worlds which operate by  alternative physical laws from our own  and interplanetary warfare for example  the protagonist of the novel  is the author lucian himself in the book  he witnesses the battle between the  people of the sun  and the people of the moon they fight  for the right to claim and colonize the  morning star  these were appointed to spin a web in  the air between the moon and the morning  star  which was done in an instant and made a  plain champagne upon which the foot  forces were planted  francois marie arue known mainly by his  pin name  voltaire wrote micromegas in 1752  in its space travel features prominently  it is also notable for suggesting that  people of other worlds  may in some ways have more advanced  technology than what is found on earth  the novel is named for one of its  characters that appears in the first of  seven chapters  micromegas is an inhabitant of one of  the planets orbiting the serious star  micromegas whose name literally means  small large  lives on a world more than 20 million  times the size of earth  and he himself is nearly 40 kilometers  tall  at age 450 micromagus is approaching the  end of his childhood micromegas  eventually travels from world to world  and a journey to develop his mind and  heart  he quickly spanned the milky way and i  am obliged to report that he never saw  throughout the stars it is made up of  the beautiful imperial sky that vikkar  der  durham  boast of having seen at the other end of  his telescope  the story describes his method of travel  as making use of the forces of repulsion  and attraction  which appears to be a clear reference to  the work of renowned scientist sir isaac  newton  it cannot be overstated the influence  that publications such as  amazing stories and weird tales had on  the sci-fi genre  these magazines often featured the work  of science fiction authors who are now  considered greats  in 1928 both magazines featured works  which essentially represented the birth  of the space opera  skylark of space by edward e smith which  i briefly mentioned in my video on  asimov's influence on the sci-fi genre  was published by amazing stories  originally in early 1928 and again  as a novel in 1946 though later versions  differ greatly from the original  publication  skylark of space depicted one of the  earliest depictions of interstellar  travel  it worked by combining a newly  discovered fictional element  known as element x with pure copper with  the final component being a field  generated by a particle accelerator  while we were unconscious miss vanaman  we had probably attained a velocity of  something like  7 billion 413 million miles per second  and that is the approximate speed at  which we are now traveling  we must be nearly six quadrillion miles  and that is a space of several hundred  light years  away from our solar system or more  plainly about six times as far away from  our earth as the north star is  we couldn't see our sun with a telescope  even if we knew which way to look for it  the same year weird tales published  edward hamilton's crashing sons  the third story in what would become  crashing sun's  interstellar patrol assembled from seven  stories in 1965  in a society that has colonized the  eight planets of our solar system  they had gone out to planet after planet  had conquered the strange atmospheres  and bacteria and gravitations until now  the races of man held sway over  all the sun's eight wheeling worlds and  it was from this earth  a thousand centuries before that there  had ventured out the first discoverers  crude little space boats whose faulty  gravity screens and uncertain controls  contrasted strangely  with the mighty leviathans that flashed  between the planets now  in the story a nearby star is swiftly  veering towards their sun  the destruction of their solar system  seems inevitable  and the current technology is not  capable of traveling to other stars in  an efficient manner  it would take them hundreds of years to  reach a new system  it is calculated by our astronomers that  in less than a year  our sun and this dying star will meet  head-on will crash together in a giant  flaming collision  and the result of that collision will be  the annihilation of our universe  for the planets of our system will  perish like flowers  in a furnace in that titanic holocaust  of crashing suns  in the story a team ends up journeying  beyond the solar system in a last-ditch  effort to save human society  now if we were to list every mention of  space travel  in every story since 300 a.d this video  will be hours long  what's important to take away from this  however is that humans have always  dreamed of traversing through the stars  essentially since forever humans  have the ability to look at the world  and see what it is  we reshape it to suit us we use the  resources around us to create new  technologies  that enrich our lives as long as there  has been a human species  we have been a species of innovators and  inventors  as we have conquered our globe one day  if we're lucky  we shall conquer others and hopefully we  as a species can learn to live in  balance with the environments that we  explore  rather than dominate them it only  requires a small leap of imagination  to look at what we have created on this  world and see the possibilities of what  we could make out there  among the stars  [Music]  you

